-- lib/machine.cub — the first-order fragment of codes, which is what compiles (PLAN.md revision 8, 9 Nov 2026, git 91167ad638c0:PLAN.md, §5.3 “The target is a data type with a meaning” and §5.4 “The reader, and why the path is by construction”).
-- Loaded after lib/specialize.cub.
-- A code is in the fragment when every construct in it has a machine form: variables, globals,
-- λ and application, let, pairs, constructors, cases and eliminations of path-free data types,
-- projections, word and primitive literals below 2^64, the word operations, the primitive
-- operations (arithmetic, conversions, loads, stores, lengths, fresh bytes), and word elimination.
-- Nothing outside it compiles: a dimension, a composition, a constructor with dimensions, a type as
-- a value, a clock or tick has no machine form, and `fragment` names the first such construct and
-- why. The fragment's meaning is the code's own (the checker's eval): there is no second syntax and
-- no second evaluator; `encode` (lib/x86.cub) prints each construct of the fragment by its
-- template.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- the fragment
frag-wrap : Verdict → Code → Verdict
frag-wrap (fo x) c = fo c
frag-wrap (not-fo r at) c = not-fo r at
